# ---------------- Platformer.events (Task 36 Part G) ----------------
# The platformer's own half of the kit: the ladder, the poses, the checkpoint and the death that
# puts the character back at it. The movement keys are not here — `include "Platformer_Controls"`
# pulls the shared controller's W (walk) and Space (jump) in, the same split FPS_Player uses. What
# a sheet adds itself goes ABOVE the include when it has to win the same key in the same tick (the
# ladder grab does), because events run top to bottom.
#
# SETUP (editor) — one scene, e.g. "PlatformerTest":
#   1. Player: a CharacterController + an EventSheet whose Event File is `Platformer`, standing on
#      a floor. Tick the EventSheet's "Configure Character Body" for the shipped capsule; the
#      ground probe uses `GroundCheckDistance` (10 cm by default), and a sheet at 0 always reads
#      airborne.
#   2. Ladder: a thin, tall BoxCollider standing against the platform it serves. The climb gate is
#      40 cm, so a character standing on the landing does not re-grab it; if yours does, move the
#      ladder 30 cm further out or lower the distance in the ladder block.
#   3. Checkpoint: any actor (an empty actor is fine) where a death should put the player back.
#   4. Optional poses: animations called Idle / Run / Jump / Fall / Climb / Death on the model.
#      Without them `anim` warns once per name and the controller still runs.
#   5. A UICanvas holding `CheckpointLabel`, so check 5 of the play-check list reads without the
#      debugger.
#
# Keys: W walks (and grabs the ladder), S lets go of the ladder, Space jumps, K kills you on
# purpose so the respawn checks need no spike or pit actor.
#
# The shared controller's speed knobs — `my WalkSpeed` 220 cm/s and `my JumpPower` 420 cm/s (a
# little over one 60 cm crate at the default gravity) — are set in `when me appears` below.

# --- the ladder: W grabs it when one is in reach ------------------------------------------
# The span ends the climb at the top, and W then walks the character off the ladder onto the
# landing. This block is above the include on purpose: `climb` registers the climb immediately, so
# the shared controller's `unless me is climbing` walk line sees it in the same tick and the climb
# and the walk never run together. Holding S (`stop climbing`) lets go.
when W is held
  unless me is climbing
  if me is on ground and me is near Ladder by 40
  climb Ladder

include "Platformer_Controls"

# --- the jump clip -------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The shared controller does the take-off (gated on `me is airborne`); this sheet only picks the
# pose, so a model without a Jump clip still jumps.
when Space is pressed
  unless me is airborne
  play anim "Jump" once

# --- letting go of the ladder -------------------------------------------------------------
# `stop climbing` keeps the height it let go at, so this is also the mid-ladder exit.
when S is pressed
  me is climbing
  stop climbing

# --- poses: ground, air, ladder -----------------------------------------------------------
# There is no `when me lands` trigger yet, so the ground states are polls: `me is on ground` and
# `me is airborne` are conditions, and `me is moving` separates a walk from standing still.
when every 0.1 seconds
  my Dead is 0
  me is climbing
  loop anim "Climb"

when every 0.1 seconds
  my Dead is 0
  me is airborne
  loop anim "Fall"

when every 0.1 seconds
  my Dead is 0
  me is on ground
  me is moving
  loop anim "Run"

when every 0.1 seconds
  my Dead is 0
  me is on ground
  unless me is moving
  loop anim "Idle"

# --- the checkpoint -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# `save checkpoint` writes this actor's own `CheckpointX` / `CheckpointY` / `CheckpointZ` /
# `CheckpointYaw`. They are ordinary per-actor variables, so a save file (Task 30) carries them and
# nothing clears them on death — only a new game, with a fresh actor, starts without one.
# `when me touches <actor>` is enter-edge, not per-tick, but a body can step out of the marker and
# back in — and a respawn puts it down right on top of one — so a bare `save checkpoint` would keep
# rewriting the same pose and printing. `my CheckpointSaved` makes the save a once-per-checkpoint
# event: set beside the pose, never cleared on death, and started at 0 by `when me appears`.
# A second marker therefore needs its own `respawn at` line and its own reset of the flag.
when me touches Checkpoint
  my CheckpointSaved is 0
  save checkpoint
  set my CheckpointSaved to 1
  print "Platformer: checkpoint saved"

# --- dying, and coming back ---------------------------------------------------------------
# K is the debug death, so the respawn checks need no hazard actor.
when K is pressed
  set my Health to 0

# `my Dead` makes the death a moment rather than a state: without the guard the block would start
# again while the wait is still running. `respawn at Checkpoint` warps through the same reset
# `teleport` uses, so moves, pushes, fall speed, the jump flags and any climb are cleared before
# the body is placed — the character comes back at rest instead of carrying its old momentum.
when my Health is lower than 1
  my Dead is 0
  set my Dead to 1
  loop anim "Death"
  wait 0.8 seconds
  respawn at Checkpoint
  set my Health to 100
  set my Dead to 0
  loop anim "Idle"
  print "Platformer: respawned at Checkpoint"

when me appears
  set my Health to 100
  set my MaxHealth to 100
  set my Dead to 0
  set my CheckpointSaved to 0
  # The shared controller reads both on every move, so they are set before the first tick.
  set my WalkSpeed to 220
  set my JumpPower to 420
  print "Platformer: W walks, Space jumps, S lets go of the ladder, K kills you"

# The checkpoint read-out for check 5: 0 before the first touch, the marker's X after it, and the
# same number again after a death and respawn.
bind CheckpointLabel to my CheckpointX format "Checkpoint X: {0}"
